```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the RIP routing daemon (ripd) on router2. The root
cause is that ripd is explicitly disabled — `ripd=no` in /etc/frr/daemons —
and the FRR configuration file (/etc/frr/frr.conf) is entirely absent on
router2. As a result, only zebra and staticd are running; no RIP process is
active.

Without ripd, router2 neither sends RIP advertisements of its directly
connected networks (1.0.0.0/8 on eth0, 128.168.0.0/16 on eth1) nor receives
RIP updates from router1. Its kernel routing table consequently contains only
three connected prefixes and zero learned routes. When pc1 (1.0.0.5) or any
Internet-lab host attempts to reach any 192.168.x.x address, router2 finds no
matching route and immediately generates "Destination Net Unreachable,"
confirmed by live ping from pc1 toward it_hod (192.168.1.2). In the reverse
direction, inbound packets from IT/CS/Office/Principle zones are forwarded by
router0 and router1 (which still carry stale RIP entries for 1.0.0.0/8 and
128.168.0.0/16) and arrive at router2, but router2 cannot route the reply
traffic back, causing the it_hod → pc1 ping to time out. Intra-zone traffic is
unaffected because it never traverses router2.

Corrective fix: On router2, set `ripd=yes` in /etc/frr/daemons, create
/etc/frr/frr.conf with a `router rip` block declaring `network 20.0.0.0/8`,
`network 1.0.0.0/8`, and `network 128.168.0.0/16` (version 2), then restart
FRR (`service frr restart`). After RIP reconverges, router2 will exchange
routes with router1 and full bidirectional reachability will be restored.
=== END ===
```